From: Davidlohr Bueso <dave@stgolabs.net>
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] block: let drivers cap the default max_sectors

Drivers can currently bound request sizes from above only through
max_hw_sectors, a hard ceiling that also takes away the
administrator's ability to raise max_sectors_kb beyond it. There is
no way to express the policy "requests above size X are possible and
sometimes profitable, but not a good default": commit 37105615f731
("block: don't reduce max_sectors based on io_opt") deliberately
stopped small io_opt values from lowering the default, and
max_user_sectors belongs to the sysfs write path.

Add queue_limits.max_default_sectors, honored only in the fallback
branch of the max_sectors computation as a driver-supplied
alternative to BLK_DEF_MAX_SECTORS_CAP. It can only lower the
default, never raise it above the cap, and an explicit user setting
(max_user_sectors) continues to take precedence up to
max_hw_sectors. Stacked devices inherit its effect through the
computed max_sectors as usual.

The immediate user is nvme-pci, which wants to stop hard-capping
max_hw_sectors at dma_opt_mapping_size() while keeping today's
default request size on translated IOMMU domains.

Signed-off-by: Davidlohr Bueso <dave@stgolabs.net>
---
 block/blk-settings.c   | 4 +++-
 include/linux/blkdev.h | 1 +
 2 files changed, 4 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/block/blk-settings.c b/block/blk-settings.c
--- a/block/blk-settings.c
+++ b/block/blk-settings.c
@@ -413,7 +413,9 @@ static int blk_validate_limits(struct queue_limits *lim)
 		lim->max_sectors =
 			min(max_hw_sectors, lim->io_min >> SECTOR_SHIFT);
 	} else {
-		lim->max_sectors = min(max_hw_sectors, BLK_DEF_MAX_SECTORS_CAP);
+		lim->max_sectors = min(max_hw_sectors,
+				min_not_zero(lim->max_default_sectors,
+					     BLK_DEF_MAX_SECTORS_CAP));
 	}
 	lim->max_sectors = round_down(lim->max_sectors,
 			logical_block_sectors);
diff --git a/include/linux/blkdev.h b/include/linux/blkdev.h
--- a/include/linux/blkdev.h
+++ b/include/linux/blkdev.h
@@ -390,6 +390,7 @@ struct queue_limits {
 	unsigned int		chunk_sectors;
 	unsigned int		max_sectors;
 	unsigned int		max_user_sectors;
+	unsigned int		max_default_sectors;
 	unsigned int		max_segment_size;
 	unsigned int		max_fast_segment_size;
 	unsigned int		physical_block_size;
